WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:05.360
“Game of Thrones” made many household names of&nbsp;
it’s stars. However, some have fallen off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.360 --> 00:00:10.720
map in one way or another despite being touched&nbsp;
by the show's massive success. Here’s the cast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.400
members that took a break from acting, and&nbsp;
why you haven't seen much of them since.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.640
Before "Game of Thrones," the child&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:22.880
actor Jack Gleeson was best known as a small boy&nbsp;
with impossibly large eyes in "Batman Begins."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.880
In the first chapter of Christopher Nolan's&nbsp;
Dark Knight Trilogy, he shares a brief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:31.920
adorable scene with the caped crusader — a&nbsp;
role credited as "Little Boy on the Balcony."

00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:37.760
In 2011, Gleeson was cast on "Game of Thrones"&nbsp;
as Joffrey Baratheon, and quickly became the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.560
character viewers loved to hate the most. We&nbsp;
watched him grow from the whiny, spiteful,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:46.880
and vengeful Prince of the Seven Kingdoms&nbsp;
into the whiny, spiteful and vengeful King&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.240
of the Seven Kingdoms. His selfish antics cause&nbsp;
the death of Arya's friend the butcher’s boy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:56.480
Sansa's direwolf Lady, and eventually the&nbsp;
most shocking death of the first season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.480 --> 00:01:01.120
the execution of former Hand of the King,&nbsp;
Ned Stark. This would turn out to be the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:06.480
act of cruelty that famously made it clear that&nbsp;
no one is safe from death on "Game of Thrones,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:09.840
not even the lead billed actor&nbsp;
in the credits. Jack Gleeson's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:14.560
face would go on to become the embodiment&nbsp;
of evil for many "Game of Thrones" fans.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:20.480
In yet another twist, King Joffrey dies abruptly&nbsp;
in Season 4 at his own wedding. As it turns out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.720
Jack Gleeson was glad to leave the role —&nbsp;
and stardom itself — behind. At the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.320
he told Entertainment Weekly,

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:30.800
"I've been acting since age 8. I just&nbsp;
stopped enjoying it as much as I used to."

00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.760
He's since stayed active in&nbsp;
the theater as a producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:38.320
co-founding the Collapsing Horse Theatre&nbsp;
Company, and attended college at Trinity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:43.200
University in Dublin. After leaving the show,&nbsp;
he only returned to television for a brief role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:49.120
on comedian Sara Pascoe's series "Out of Her&nbsp;
Mind." Even so, that was just a sort of meta&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.120 --> 00:01:53.920
commentary on the villainous role that&nbsp;
led him to turn away from the spotlight.

00:01:54.987 --> 00:01:58.880
Isaac Hempstead Wright landed the role of Bran&nbsp;
Stark in "Game of Thrones" at just 12 years old.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.880 --> 00:02:04.080
His prior onscreen experience was a small part&nbsp;
in the horror movie "The Awakening" that had yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.080 --> 00:02:10.160
to be released. As Bran, he arguably instigated&nbsp;
a large portion of the plot of the entire show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.720
since his discovery of Jamie and Cersei's&nbsp;
Lannister's incestuous affair. This led to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:19.600
his serious fall, thanks to Jamie, which&nbsp;
was the inciting incident of the pilot.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:21.840
“The things I do for love.”

00:02:22.960 --> 00:02:27.520
Bran would go on to weave one of the most unique&nbsp;
journeys through Westeros over the course of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:32.640
show. Paralyzed from the waist down, he learns&nbsp;
to adapt to his new life just in time to escape&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.640 --> 00:02:38.480
to the North when Winterfell is captured by Theon&nbsp;
Greyjoy. He then hones his ability to "warg" into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:43.520
his direwolf, and is eventually trained by&nbsp;
a mystical man known as the Three Eyed Raven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.720
in the art of greensight, which essentially means&nbsp;
having visions of the past. This enables him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.720 --> 00:02:53.840
discern the "Game of Thrones" twist that most&nbsp;
of the audience saw coming from a mile away.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:59.120
That is, Jon Snow's true parentage as an&nbsp;
heir to the Iron Throne. In a shocking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:03.600
turn that even Hempstead Wright thought was&nbsp;
a joke at first, Bran ends up on the throne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:09.120
at the story's end. He also winds up with a&nbsp;
mouthful of a title, as Tyrion says in full,

00:03:09.120 --> 00:03:11.360
"Bran the Broken, the First of His Name,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:17.040
King of the Andals and the First Men, Lord of&nbsp;
the Six Kingdoms, and Protector of the Realm."

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:20.720
Isaac Hempstead Wright, meanwhile, was&nbsp;
struggling to find normalcy behind the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:26.720
scenes while his alter ego took such strange&nbsp;
turns on a huge hit TV show. He told Esquire UK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:30.480
that "Game of Thrones" all but ruined&nbsp;
his college experience. He shared,

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:35.040
"My address got published [...] I couldn't walk&nbsp;
out of my halls without having to take a selfie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.080
Eventually I got assigned&nbsp;
a campus police officer."

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:43.040
He dropped out, but eventually returned to&nbsp;
University College London to study neuroscience.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:45.680
Focusing on his studies, he&nbsp;
naturally took a break from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:52.480
acting until appearing in a minor role in&nbsp;
the 2021 film "Voyagers" in April of 2021.

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:54.960
Jacob Anderson gained prominence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.960 --> 00:03:59.440
with recurring roles on "Broadchurch" and&nbsp;
"Episodes" before landing the role of Grey Worm.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:04.080
As the elected leader of the Unsullied, a&nbsp;
group of eunuch warriors raised as slaves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:08.160
he displays a fierce devotion to&nbsp;
Daenerys Targaryen for freeing him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.200
and all of his brethren. He chooses to fight&nbsp;
for her as one of her most trusted lieutenants.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:19.040
Eventually, he has time amidst all the late-season&nbsp;
chaos for a doomed romance with Daenerys' advisor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.040 --> 00:04:24.560
Missandei, and sails with the remaining Unsullied&nbsp;
to Missandei's homeland, at the end of the series.

00:04:24.560 --> 00:04:28.320
Anderson didn't do much other acting&nbsp;
during his time on "Game of Thrones,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.880
and hasn't appeared onscreen since the series&nbsp;
ended. But don't worry that casting directors are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:37.360
giving this talented actor the cold shoulder. He's&nbsp;
stepped out of the film and television spotlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:42.560
because he's focusing on his music career,&nbsp;
as a pop/R&amp;B singer known as Raleigh Ritchie.

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.920
Few characters during all of "Game of Thrones"&nbsp;
were more compelling — and few deaths more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.880
heart-wrenching – than Shireen Baratheon. The&nbsp;
only surviving child of Stannis Baratheon,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.880 --> 00:04:57.760
she's treated coldly by her father as he pursues&nbsp;
the Iron Throne after his brother's death.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.760 --> 00:05:01.280
She doesn't get any more warmth from&nbsp;
her mother, who resents her for being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:05.920
partially disfigured from a disease known&nbsp;
as greyscale. She finds comfort, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.920 --> 00:05:09.920
in her friendship with the kind Ser&nbsp;
Davos, who is in her father's service.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.200
Shireen even demonstrates ingenuity at multiple&nbsp;
points that aid her father in his various battles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.200 --> 00:05:21.440
and quests across the series. Ultimately, however,&nbsp;
he's convinced by the sorceress Melisandre during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:26.480
a desperate moment that a sacrifice of "King's&nbsp;
blood" will help turn the tide in his favor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.480 --> 00:05:31.200
and Shireen is his only option. In a scene&nbsp;
that many fans found unnecessarily cruel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.280
poor Shireen is burned alive as her&nbsp;
mother cries in anguish and Stannis&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:40.000
looks away in tears. This show really&nbsp;
could get pretty dark when it wanted to.

00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:44.800
The English actress Kerry Ingram brought Shireen&nbsp;
to life, and unlike her onscreen counterpart,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.120
she remains thankfully alive and well.&nbsp;
Already the winner of an Olivier Award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:53.360
for starring in "Matilda: the Musical" before&nbsp;
"Game of Thrones," she made a few other TV&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:58.480
appearances. She was actually a series regular&nbsp;
on "Free Rein" during the run of "Thrones," but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.480 --> 00:06:04.240
hasn't had any appearances since it ended&nbsp;
in 2019. The pandemic likely played a role,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:07.360
but we'll surely be hearing&nbsp;
from Kerry Ingram in the future.

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:12.800
Luke Barnes is primarily a British playwright&nbsp;
and screenwriter, with only a single acting role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:18.000
as "Drunk Man" in an episode of "Skins" to his&nbsp;
name before playing Rast on "Game of Thrones."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:22.400
Nevertheless, he left an indelible mark&nbsp;
as one of several unsavory villains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:26.640
Jon Snow encounters during his time at&nbsp;
Castle Black with the Night's Watch.

00:06:26.640 --> 00:06:30.800
Rast is a foul criminal that "took the&nbsp;
black" and pledged service to the Night's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:35.520
Watch instead of a worse punishment. He&nbsp;
torments Jon's friend Sam during training,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:41.120
and acts clearly envious of Jon's skill and&nbsp;
increasingly prominent role as the months pass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:46.720
He stabs the honorable Lord Commander Mormont&nbsp;
in the back during the mutiny at Craster's Keep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:51.200
and later even tortures poor Hodor&nbsp;
when the mutineers briefly detain him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:56.240
along with Bran Stark. His eventual death&nbsp;
at the paws of Jon Snow's direwolf Ghost is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.240 --> 00:07:01.440
one of several very satisfying ones over the&nbsp;
course of this extremely death-friendly show.

00:07:01.440 --> 00:07:04.880
After wrapping his time on "Game of&nbsp;
Thrones," Barnes did have a couple small&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.880 --> 00:07:09.360
parts in short films, but hasn't appeared&nbsp;
onscreen since the show ended. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.080
he's returned to his native Northern Ireland to&nbsp;
focus on writing and producing theater once more.

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:20.000
Like many of the other child actors on "Game of&nbsp;
Thrones," Ben Hawkey had just a few small roles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.200
before appearing as Hot Pie, friend of Arya Stark.

00:07:23.200 --> 00:07:27.760
An orphan who's been sent to the Night's Watch&nbsp;
when they meet, he bonds with Arya as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:32.480
Gendry when they're waylaid by Lannister&nbsp;
forces and held prisoner at Harrenhal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.480 --> 00:07:37.200
Eventually they escape, only to be captured by&nbsp;
the Brotherhood Without Banners and escorted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:42.960
the Inn at the Crossroads. There, Hot Pie finds&nbsp;
a rare happy ending for a minor "Game of Thrones"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:48.160
character. He becomes a baker's apprentice,&nbsp;
taken on by the owner of the inn to bake bread.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:52.960
He even gives Arya a loaf of bread shaped&nbsp;
like a direwolf as a goodbye present.

00:07:52.960 --> 00:07:58.160
Ben Hawkey hasn’t been seen acting onscreen since&nbsp;
his time on "Game of Thrones," although, he has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:03.600
apparently turned his role as Hot Pie into a new&nbsp;
real-life business. As reported by US Weekly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:07.440
he's partnered with a U.K. delivery&nbsp;
service to bring the direwolf bread to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.440 --> 00:08:11.352
the public with a business incredibly&nbsp;
named "You Know Nothing, Jon Dough."

00:08:12.560 --> 00:08:17.200
British actor Stephen Cole was primarily&nbsp;
known for five years on the long-running&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:23.360
Liverpool soap opera "Brookside,"&nbsp;
playing Leo Johnson from 1996 to 2001.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.360 --> 00:08:29.680
This was before being cast as Kovarro in Season&nbsp;
2 of "Game of Thrones" in 2012. After the death&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.880
of Khal Drogo at the end of Season 1, Kovarro&nbsp;
becomes one of Daenerys Targaryen's most trusted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:39.920
bloodriders as she navigates the desert of the&nbsp;
Red Waste and the treacherous city of Qarth.

00:08:39.920 --> 00:08:43.760
After playing a semi-prominent part in&nbsp;
that storyline in Season 2, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.960
Kovarro never appears again for unexplained&nbsp;
reasons. Steven Cole would have minor parts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.960 --> 00:08:55.120
in 2014 and 2018, but has yet to act&nbsp;
since "Game of Thrones" ended in 2019.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.120 --> 00:08:59.200
It's not known if the pandemic or personal&nbsp;
reasons have kept him off screens.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.200 --> 00:09:03.360
Out of a cast of hundreds, it's possible&nbsp;
there's at least one that just has bum luck.

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:08.240
Last but not least, we have arguably&nbsp;
the biggest villain of the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.240 --> 00:09:12.800
show — the Night King. Commanding an army&nbsp;
of white walkers, wights, and eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:17.600
even his own dragon, the Night King slowly&nbsp;
and surely marches south to invade Westeros.

00:09:18.880 --> 00:09:19.680
“Don’t be afraid.”

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:25.120
This culminates with the epic battle in&nbsp;
the Season 8 episode "The Long Night."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:28.720
Though the Night King was originally&nbsp;
played by character actor Richard Brake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.720 --> 00:09:33.040
he left the show after Season 6 due to a&nbsp;
scheduling conflict. He was replaced by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:38.080
stuntman Vladimir Furdik for Seasons 7 and&nbsp;
8. It probably helped the transition that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:43.200
the character's face is a combination of&nbsp;
makeup and CGI, and he also never speaks.

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:46.080
Furdik has been a stuntman&nbsp;
for decades in Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.640
working on movies like "Dragonheart," "Behind&nbsp;
Enemy Lines," and "Thor: The Dark World."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:55.200
In addition to appearing in five episodes as&nbsp;
the Night King, he worked as a stunt performer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.200 --> 00:10:00.400
on 16 episodes of "Game of Thrones" as well.&nbsp;
He is also credited as a stunt supervisor on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.400 --> 00:10:04.800
the episode "Winterfell." He continues his&nbsp;
work as a stunt performer on the Netflix&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:09.360
series "The Witcher," but the Night King&nbsp;
remains his last credited on-camera role.

